---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CURRENCY_META, fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { dataFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const { riskMetrics, correlations, signals, btcHistory, btcDaily } = dataFor(currency);
const meta = CURRENCY_META[currency];

/**
 * Figures quoted below come from the committed data, not from prose. Every
 * number on this page that could drift is read from the file that produced it
 * — the alternative is a methodology page that describes a version of the
 * pipeline that no longer runs, which is worse than having none.
 */
const volWindows = riskMetrics.rollingVol.map((w) => w.windowDays).join('/');
const corrWindow = correlations.windowDays;
const t = signals.thresholds;
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · methodology"
  description="Every formula the site computes, the source each reads, and what each one deliberately does not claim."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Methodology</h1>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>How to read this</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Every figure on this site is computed by a pure function in <code>pipeline/</code>, unit-tested
      against fixed fixtures with exact expected outputs, and committed to <code>/data</code> as
      versioned JSON by a scheduled job. Pages render that file and do no arithmetic of their own.
      This page is the whole set of formulas in one place; each section also carries its own note.
      Figures quoted here are read from the committed data, so they cannot describe a pipeline that
      no longer runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <dl class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Daily close</dt>
        <dd class="num">CoinGecko</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">{btcDaily.series.length} days · to {btcDaily.stats.latestDate}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Deep history</dt>
        <dd class="num">blockchain.com</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">{btcHistory.series.length} days · from {btcHistory.series[0]?.date}</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Benchmarks</dt>
        <dd class="num">Yahoo / FRED</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">S&amp;P 500, gold, DXY</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>Rolling vol reads</dt>
        <dd class="num">{riskMetrics.rollingVolSource}</dd>
        <dd class="sub num">full history, not the 365d window</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="method-note">
      Two BTC price sources, deliberately. CoinGecko's keyless tier caps history at 365 days, which
      is enough for the risk window but not for halving cycles or an all-time high, so a second
      series carries the full history. They disagree by a fraction of a percent on any given day —
      different exchange sets, different snapshot times — so each figure is labelled with the source
      it read rather than the two being quietly reconciled. Where both could serve, the deeper
      series wins.{fxNote(currency)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Returns and volatility</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Daily return</strong> is the log return <code>ln(Pt / Pt−1)</code>. Log rather than
      simple because they sum over time, which is what makes an annualisation meaningful.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Realised volatility</strong> over a window is the sample standard deviation of those
      log returns, annualised by <code>√365</code> — 365 and not 252, because BTC trades every day.
      Benchmarks annualise by <code>√252</code> for the same reason in reverse. Windows: {volWindows} days.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      <strong>Sharpe</strong> is mean return over standard deviation, annualised, at a zero risk-free
      rate — stated rather than assumed, since a non-zero rate would change the ranking between
      assets. <strong>Sortino</strong> replaces the denominator with downside deviation, counting
      only negative returns. Both are null rather than zero when undefined, but for different
      reasons: Sharpe when there are fewer than two returns or the variance is zero, Sortino when
      there are no negative returns at all to form a downside deviation from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Drawdown</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      <code>Pt / max(P0..Pt) − 1</code>, per day, against the <em>running</em> peak rather than a
      fixed one — so a drawdown is always measured from the highest close seen up to that day, and
      is zero on any day that sets a new high. The headline maximum keeps the first date it was
      reached, not the last.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Halving cycles</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Each cycle is indexed by days since its halving and normalised to a multiple of that day's
      close, which is what makes four cycles comparable on one axis. Cycles have different lengths
      and the current one is incomplete, so a chart position late in the x-axis legitimately shows
      fewer than four lines — an absent cycle is omitted rather than drawn at zero.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Correlation</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Pearson correlation of daily log returns over a rolling {corrWindow}-day window. Two
      corrections matter more than the formula:
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      BTC closes at 00:00 UTC while equities and metals close at 21:00 UTC the previous session, so
      BTC is re-dated to the session it actually shares before pairing. Without that, BTC's −49.7%
      on the snapshot dated 13 March 2020 met the S&amp;P's +9.29% rebound while its flat 12th met the
      S&amp;P's −9.51%: one inverted outlier pair then dominated a 60-observation window for three
      months, and the site reported BTC and equities as <em>inverse</em> through the most famous
      co-crash on record. Correcting the offset moves the trailing correlation from +0.09 to +0.44.
      And the re-dating happens <em>before</em> currency conversion, not after: conversion is itself
      a per-date join, so re-dating afterwards leaves the two legs on different days and the FX term
      stops cancelling — measured at 0.16 of noise in GBP correlations, against a 0.25 threshold.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Signals</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Band states are hysteretic. A band is entered only once {t.confirmDays} consecutive
      observations agree, and is then dated to the first of them — the day the change began, not the
      day it became certain. On this {meta.label} series a bare threshold test would give{' '}
      {signals.rawSpans.vol} volatility spans and {signals.rawSpans.drawdown} drawdown spans over the
      same window; confirmation is what turns that into a state worth reporting. A candidate that
      has not yet earned the switch is published as pending rather than hidden.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      Volatility bands: below {t.volLowPct}% and above {t.volHighPct}% annualised, on the{' '}
      {t.volWindowDays}-day window. Drawdown bands: {t.drawdownBandsPct.join('%, ')}% from the
      running peak, deepest reached. The same confirmation machine drives the correlation regimes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>DCA and holdings</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      The simulator buys at the close on each scheduled date, less a per-buy fee; a date missing
      from the data buys on the next available day, month-end schedules clamp, and two schedules
      landing together collapse into one buy. Undeployed cash counts toward wealth so both
      strategies start from the same budget and the comparison is like-for-like. Taxes, slippage and
      yield on idle cash are ignored.
    </p>
    <p class="method-note">
      A holdings line holds the BTC amount <em>constant</em> across history. The site does not know
      when any of it was bought, so this is what that much BTC was worth — not a reconstruction of a
      position. A line implying a purchase history the data cannot support would be worse than no
      line. What you enter stays in your browser; see the note on the holdings page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="chart-section">
    <h2>Rounding, and what is deliberately not rounded</h2>
    <p class="method-note">
      Rounding is applied at the serialization edge — 2 dp for percentages and money, 8 dp for BTC,
      4 dp for cycle multiples — never mid-calculation. Two series are kept unrounded on purpose:
      converted closes, because a 2 dp round on a sub-pound 2010 price is an error of up to 11% that
      would propagate into the monthly heatmap; and the holdings value series, because rounding
      zeroes every early day for a small stack and a log axis then drops them off the chart.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
